pySMESH: Cartesian_3D with viscous layers binds every node of its offset VERTEXes and EDGEs to
a layer edge, and a failure leaves no mesh behind (SMESH V9_16_0; applies after
StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL_group_2d.patch).

Defect: StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL::ViscousBuilder::MakeViscousLayers fails on inclined walls with
"Viscous layers construction error: bad mesh on offset geometry", and leaves the copied
offset mesh in the target.

Symptom: a regular hexagonal prism of circumradius 1 and height 1, layers on every face
(0.2 thick, 3 layers, factor 1.2), grid spacing 0.1: the compute fails. With spacing 0.13 the
same shape meshes. Rotating the hexagon moves the failing corners with it.

Root cause, measured with an instrumented build: makePrisms returns false on a boundary face
node that has no layer edge (StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL.cxx:466-467). The node lies on an offset
VERTEX or EDGE at a corner where the shape touches the grid's bounding box along one vertical
edge, that is where an end plane of the grid lies. There the Cartesian offset mesh makes two
nodes at each point of that EDGE and its VERTEXes, a grid node and an intersection node. Then:
* At a VERTEX both copies survive. The boundary faces use one, and the projection to the
  initial shape binds the other (SMESH_Algo::VertexNode).
* Along the EDGE the duplicate grid nodes are removed, but Hexahedron::addSegments found no
  ordered node chain on the doubled EDGE and made no segment. projectToEdge skips an EDGE with
  no element (:202), so its nodes, which the boundary faces use, got no layer edge.
On failure the error goes to the sub-mesh of the compound Cartesian_3D receives (:1269-1278),
which a caller does not walk, after copyMesh (:1267) has filled the target with the offset
mesh.

Change, in StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL.cxx only:
* Before the projection, coincident nodes (within Precision::Confusion) on the offset mesh's
  VERTEX and EDGE sub-meshes are merged with SMESH_MeshEditor::MergeNodes.
* projectToEdge skips an EDGE only when it has no node, as setBnd2Sub already does (:669).
* On a failure of the prisms, the target mesh is cleared and the error is set on every SOLID
  of the shape too.

Not changed: a node of an offset FACE, and every mesh with no such duplicates and no EDGE
without segments (bit for bit).
diff --git a/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL.cxx b/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL.cxx
--- a/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL.cxx
+++ b/src/StdMeshers/StdMeshers_Cartesian_VL.cxx
@@ -199,7 +199,10 @@
   {
     SMESHDS_SubMesh* sm = theOffsetMDS->MeshElements( theEOS._offsetShape );
  
-    if ( !sm || sm->NbElements() == 0 )
+    // pySMESH: test the nodes, which are all this function projects. The Cartesian offset
+    // mesh can leave an EDGE that a grid end plane passes through with nodes but no segment;
+    // skipping it left the boundary faces on those nodes with no layer edge.
+    if ( !sm || sm->NbNodes() == 0 )
       return;
 
     int addVertexNode = createVertex ? 1 : 0;
@@ -1132,6 +1135,27 @@
   if ( isCanceled() ) return false; // pySMESH: canceled
   initMDS->ClearMesh(); // avoid mesh superposition on multiple calls of addLayers
   offsetMDS->SetAllCellsNotMarked();
+
+  // pySMESH: the Cartesian offset mesh can carry two nodes at one point of an offset VERTEX
+  // or EDGE, where an end plane of the grid passes through it: the boundary faces use one,
+  // and the projection below binds the other (VertexNode, the EDGE's own nodes), so makePrisms
+  // finds a face node with no layer edge and fails. Merge them first.
+  {
+    TIDSortedNodeSet onVertexOrEdge;
+    TopTools_IndexedMapOfShape offsetVE;
+    TopExp::MapShapes( shrinkGeomToMesh, TopAbs_VERTEX, offsetVE );
+    TopExp::MapShapes( shrinkGeomToMesh, TopAbs_EDGE,   offsetVE );
+    for ( int i = 1; i <= offsetVE.Extent(); ++i )
+      if ( SMESHDS_SubMesh* sm = offsetMDS->MeshElements( offsetVE( i )))
+        for ( SMDS_NodeIteratorPtr nIt = sm->GetNodes(); nIt->more(); )
+          onVertexOrEdge.insert( nIt->next() );
+    SMESH_MeshEditor offsetEditor( &offsetMesh );
+    SMESH_MeshEditor::TListOfListOfNodes coincident;
+    offsetEditor.FindCoincidentNodes( onVertexOrEdge, Precision::Confusion(), coincident,
+                                      /*SeparateCornersAndMedium=*/false );
+    if ( !coincident.empty() )
+      offsetEditor.MergeNodes( coincident );
+  }
 
   TopTools_IndexedMapOfShape shrinkGeomMap;
   TopExp::MapShapes( shrinkGeomToMesh, shrinkGeomMap );
@@ -1279,12 +1303,20 @@
 
   if ( !prismsOk )
   {
-    if ( SMESH_subMesh * sm = theMesh.GetSubMesh( theShape ))
-    {
-      sm->GetComputeError() =
-        SMESH_ComputeError::New( COMPERR_ALGO_FAILED,
-                                 "Viscous layers construction error: bad mesh on offset geometry" );
-    }
+    // pySMESH: leave no half-copied mesh behind, and put the error on every SOLID as well:
+    // Cartesian_3D receives a compound of its solids, whose sub-mesh a caller does not walk.
+    initMDS->ClearMesh();
+    std::vector< TopoDS_Shape > failed( 1, theShape );
+    for ( TopExp_Explorer soExp( theShape, TopAbs_SOLID ); soExp.More(); soExp.Next() )
+      if ( !soExp.Current().IsSame( theShape ))
+        failed.push_back( soExp.Current() );
+    for ( const TopoDS_Shape& s : failed )
+      if ( SMESH_subMesh * sm = theMesh.GetSubMesh( s ))
+      {
+        sm->GetComputeError() =
+          SMESH_ComputeError::New( COMPERR_ALGO_FAILED,
+                                   "Viscous layers construction error: bad mesh on offset geometry" );
+      }
     return prismsOk;
   }
 
